    "body": "Photo: VesselFinder\nBy Angelo Mathais India correspondent\n29 October 2026\nIntra-Asia and Middle East short-haul trades are seen to be the most sought-after launch pad or entry point for aspiring container shipping entrepreneurs, as the industry becomes more competitive and challenging in an environment of ever-growing trade volatility.\nThe ‘newest kid on the block’ is Jade Pacific Line (JPL), a company established by a cohort of former executives of SeaLead Shipping, which ceased operations earlier this year and in liquidation, the victim of US sanctions action over its alleged links to Iran.\nJPL was incorporated as a Singapore-based entity in April with former SeaLead chief commercial office Doreen Yeo named as chief executive, according to available information.\nThe new operator has announced the launch of a shuttle loop connecting India and the Red Sea, injecting more capacity into a trade that has already been a target for opportunistic carriers.\nWhile the exact service specifics, like capacity and sailing frequency, have not been disclosed officially, The Loadstar understands the port rotation will be Mundra-Nhava Sheva-Karachi-Sokhna-Mundra.\nThe 1,118 teu Medkon Sia is said to be the lone vessel deployed on the loop. It had its inaugural call at JNPA on 4 October and is expected to make its next call at Mundra today, sources said.\nThey believe the newcomer wants to assess market response before deciding to add more ships for a fixed schedule rotation, either fortnightly or weekly.\nThey see the intended coverage as a strategic move by JPL, as trades exposed to operational and security risks typically offer potential for higher freight yields.\nAccording to the latest India-Middle East price index from Container Trades Statistics, freight rates have soared this year following the outbreak of conflict, eschewing the stability seen in 2025 – its August price index was up more than 250% year on year.\nSource: Container Trades Statistics\n“Regional shipping networks are not shaped only by the largest global carriers,” said Sean Fang, senior consultant at China-based Way-Way International Logistics.\n“Smaller operators can also create additional links between specific origin and destination markets, particularly where regional cargo demand supports a more-focused service.” he added in a media post.\nCapacity released by SeaLead had largely been captured by emerging Chinese liner CULines, which has consolidated regional operations alongside the predominant intra-Asia regional carriers.\nSignalling its growth plans, CULines has swiftly expanded its fleet to more than 50 containerships, and recently signed a major order for two 14,000 teu newbuildings at China’s Hudong-Zhonghua Shipbuilding.\nThe wider intra-Asia trades are undoubtedly crowded with carriers, but their underlying resilient demand strengths continue to attract capacity participants.\nWhile the region’s core growth appeal lies in the concentration of production centres, accelerating supply chain diversification, the region is delivering another source of intra-regional cargo opportunities for ocean capacity stakeholders, industry experts believe.\nYou can contact the writer at [email protected]",
